PG.notes("s07-cascade", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains how the browser resolves conflicting CSS rules through four ranked categories: position, specificity, type and importance. Includes three quick quiz questions and an exercise that recolours nested boxes using only CSS, with no HTML changes.</p>\n<h3>What the cascade is</h3>\n<ul><li>The cascade only matters when several rules target the same element and set the same property. The browser must decide which declaration wins; the rest are ignored.</li><li>Every matching rule is applied in turn, so a later or stronger one overwrites an earlier one. What the user sees is the result left at the end.</li><li>Worked example: li { color: green } in the external stylesheet turns an ordered list green; adding style=&quot;color: red&quot; to one li makes just that item red, because inline styles come last in the process.</li><li>Four categories decide the winner, listed from weakest to strongest: position, specificity, type, importance. Inside each category there is a smaller ranking of its own.</li></ul>\n<h3>1. Position</h3>\n<ul><li>When two declarations are equal in every other respect, the one written lower in the file wins.</li><li>This applies inside one rule block (setting color twice, the second sticks) and across rules (a second li selector further down overrides the first).</li><li>It holds in both external stylesheets and internal &lt;style&gt; blocks.</li></ul>\n<h3>2. Specificity</h3>\n<ul><li>Specificity measures how narrowly a selector aims at an element. Take an li with id=&quot;first-id&quot;, class=&quot;first-class&quot; and the draggable attribute: four different selectors can all hit it.</li><li>Ranking from least to most specific as taught: element selector (li), class selector (.first-class), attribute selector (li[draggable]), id selector (#first-id).</li><li>An id is the strongest because a page should hold only one element with a given id, so it points to a single element. A class can be shared by many elements.</li><li>The lecture ranks attribute above class because in practice it overrode the class in her demo. (Extra) The official specificity rules give class, attribute and pseudo-class selectors the same weight, so ties between them fall back to position. Do not rely on attribute beating class.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A common mental scoring is inline 1000, id 100, class/attribute 10, element 1, and the highest total wins. Selectors are really compared column by column rather than added in base 10, but the model is fine for everyday use.</li><li>If all four selectors were written and each set a different colour, the most specific one (the id rule, orange in her example) is what the page shows.</li></ul>\n<h3>3. Type (where the CSS lives)</h3>\n<ul><li>External stylesheet via &lt;link&gt;, internal CSS in a &lt;style&gt; element, and inline CSS in a style attribute.</li><li>Strength order: inline beats internal, which beats external.</li><li>Reasoning: inline targets a single element, internal covers one page, external may be shared by many pages.</li><li>The browser conceptually checks external first, then internal, then inline, with each later layer overwriting the previous.</li></ul>\n<h3>4. Importance (!important)</h3>\n<ul><li>Add !important after the value, before the semicolon, to make that declaration beat position, specificity and type.</li><li>It overrides even an inline style or an id selector, and works wherever the rule sits in the file.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Use it sparingly: once several rules carry it, you are back to fighting over order and specificity, and debugging gets harder.</li></ul>\n<h3>Using the cascade as a debugging checklist</h3>\n<ul><li>When a style is not showing, walk the categories from the top: is something marked !important, is an inline style overriding, is a more specific selector winning, is a later rule overriding?</li><li>Treat the summary diagram as a reference to keep beside you rather than something to memorise.</li></ul>\n<h3>Quiz answers (worked through)</h3>\n<ul><li>Quiz 1: an h1 with a class and an id, two external rules, one by class (blue) and one by id (green). Specificity decides, the id wins, result green.</li><li>Quiz 2: an h1 with two classes (an element can carry several, separated by spaces), and two class rules of equal specificity. Position decides, the lower rule wins, result blue.</li><li>Quiz 3: an h1 with an id rule in CSS and also an inline style. Type outranks specificity, so inline wins, result blue.</li><li>Lesson from quiz 3: a rule that wins in one category can still lose to a higher-ranked category, so keep the order of the big categories in mind.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Position: later declaration wins</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">li {\n  color: red;\n  color: blue;   /* blue wins, it is lower */\n}\n\nli {\n  color: green;  /* lower still, green wins */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Specificity ladder for one element</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">/* &lt;li id=&quot;first-id&quot; class=&quot;first-class&quot; draggable=&quot;true&quot;&gt; */\nli { color: red; }\n.first-class { color: blue; }\nli[draggable] { color: purple; }\n#first-id { color: orange; }   /* wins */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The three types of CSS and !important</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;style.css&quot;&gt; &lt;!-- external, weakest --&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;h1 { color: green; }&lt;/style&gt;      &lt;!-- internal --&gt;\n&lt;h1 style=&quot;color: blue;&quot;&gt;Hi&lt;/h1&gt;         &lt;!-- inline, strongest of the three --&gt;\n\n&lt;style&gt;h1 { color: red !important; }&lt;/style&gt; &lt;!-- beats the inline style --&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: 7.1 CSS Cascade: recolour the nested boxes</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html has an outer div with id outer-box and class box, which contains two div.box wrappers, each holding a yellow paragraph and an inner div with classes box and inner-box that holds a paragraph with class white-text. style.css already has rules for .box (blue background, 10px padding) and p (yellow, no margin or padding). HTML and existing CSS must stay unchanged.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Match the goal image: white text in the innermost paragraphs, red inner boxes, and a purple outer box, with 10px padding on every box.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add a rule for .white-text setting color to white.</li><li>Add a rule for .inner-box setting background-color to red. Place it below the .box rule, otherwise the later .box rule overrides it.</li><li>Add a rule for #outer-box setting background-color to purple. The id is the only thing that distinguishes this div from the other .box divs.</li><li>Do not add padding: all three boxes already share the class box, which provides the 10px.</li><li>Compare the preview with the goal image.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Purple outer container, blue middle boxes, red inner boxes; yellow text in the middle boxes, white text in the red ones.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.box {\n  background-color: blue;\n  padding: 10px;\n}\n\n#outer-box {\n  background-color: purple;\n}\n\n.inner-box {\n  background-color: red;\n}\n\np {\n  color: yellow;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n\n.white-text {\n  color: white;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting .inner-box above .box: both are class selectors, so the later .box blue overrides red.</li><li>Expecting a p rule to beat .white-text just because p is written lower. Here .white-text is more specific than the element selector p, so it wins wherever it sits.</li><li>Adding extra padding to #outer-box changes the result, because the id rule outranks .box.</li><li>Overusing !important to force styles instead of fixing selector order or specificity.</li><li>Assuming specificity outranks everything: inline styles beat id selectors.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s07-combining", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to build precise CSS selectors by combining simpler ones: grouping with commas, direct-child with &gt;, descendant with a space, and chaining with no space. Five small exercises on a to-do list page practise each combination, with the HTML locked.</p>\n<h3>Why combine selectors</h3>\n<ul><li>Adding a class to every element that needs a different style clutters the HTML and does not scale. Combining selectors lets CSS aim at an element using its surroundings or its multiple attributes instead.</li><li>Example from the previous exercise: instead of putting white-text on the paragraph, target any p that sits inside .inner-box with the rule .inner-box p.</li></ul>\n<h3>Group: comma</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: selector1, selector2 { ... }. Each selector is separated by a comma, and the one block of declarations applies to every element matched by any of them.</li><li>Works with any selector type: elements, classes, ids, or a mix, and with two or more selectors.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Splitting onto one selector per line is a common formatting habit. A comma-separated list is equivalent to writing the rules out separately.</li></ul>\n<h3>Child: &gt;</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: parent &gt; child. The left side is the parent, the right side must be a direct child, one generation down.</li><li>Grandchildren and deeper do not match. In the exercise, div.box has two children (a p and a ul), while the li items inside the ul are grandchildren.</li><li>Used to separate a paragraph inside .box from similar paragraphs elsewhere on the page.</li></ul>\n<h3>Descendant: space</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: ancestor descendant, with a single space. The right side matches if any ancestor at any depth satisfies the left side.</li><li>.box li hits the list items even though they are two levels below .box. .box &gt; li would match nothing here.</li><li>Preferred by the instructor over &gt; unless you really need the strict parent-child relationship, since it is broader and usually what you want. Both are valid.</li><li>Useful on large pages: restricting to a container prevents the rule from leaking onto unrelated li elements elsewhere.</li></ul>\n<h3>Chaining: no space</h3>\n<ul><li>Writing selectors directly against each other means one element must satisfy all of them, e.g. h1#title.big.heading is an h1 with id title and both classes big and heading.</li><li>Selectors that carry a prefix (. for class, # for id) can follow each other in any order, but a type (element) selector has to come first. Otherwise .doneli would be read as a single class name.</li><li>Compare li.done (one li that has class done) with li .done (an element with class done inside an li). A stray space completely changes the meaning.</li><li>Chaining is the way to separate li.done from p.done when the HTML cannot be edited.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mixing combinations</h3>\n<ul><li>All of the above can be stacked: a space for ancestry, chaining for the target element, and commas to group several such selectors.</li><li>Read the selector as a plain sentence to check it, e.g. ul p.done means a p with class done that sits somewhere inside a ul.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Hovering over a selector in the DevTools Styles panel highlights the elements it matches, a quick way to verify a selector.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">The four ways to combine selectors</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">h1, h2 { color: blueviolet; }       /* group */\n.box &gt; p { color: firebrick; }      /* child: direct children only */\n.box li { color: blue; }            /* descendant: any depth */\nli.done { color: seagreen; }        /* chained: li AND class done */</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Combined: ancestor plus chained target</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">ul p.done {\n  font-size: 0.5rem;\n}\n/* alternative using child, valid here because the p is a direct child of ul */\nul &gt; p.done {\n  font-size: 0.5rem;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">A very specific chain</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">h1#title.big.heading {\n  color: black;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: 7.2 Combining Selectors: to-do list styling</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html (do not change it) has an h1 and h2, a div.box containing p.done and ul.list with three li (two with class done), then a separate ul that holds a p.done, and a final plain paragraph. style.css is empty apart from a comment.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Style five specific groups of elements using only selector combinations.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Group h1 and h2 in one rule with colour blueviolet.</li><li>Make only the paragraph that is a direct child of .box firebrick, using the child combinator. Other paragraphs stay untouched.</li><li>Make the three bullet items blue using the descendant combinator from .box, not the bare li selector. A child combinator would fail because the li items are two levels deep.</li><li>Make the two li elements that carry class done seagreen by chaining li.done (no space, element first).</li><li>Shrink the p.done inside the standalone ul to font-size 0.5rem: use ul p.done, or ul &gt; p.done.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Heading and sub-heading in blueviolet; the intro paragraph in the box firebrick; li items blue, with the two done items overriding to seagreen; the odd paragraph inside the stray ul tiny. The final plain paragraph keeps defaults.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">h1,\nh2 {\n  color: blueviolet;\n}\n\n.box &gt; p {\n  color: firebrick;\n}\n\n.box li {\n  color: blue;\n}\n\nli.done {\n  color: seagreen;\n}\n\nul p.done {\n  font-size: 0.5rem;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Adding a space inside a chain (li .done) when you meant li.done, or putting the class first (.doneli).</li><li>Using &gt; when the target is deeper than one level.</li><li>Using a bare li or p.done selector, which also hits elements the exercise wants left alone.</li><li>Editing the HTML to add classes, which the exercises forbid.</li><li>Rule order: .box li (blue) is written before li.done (seagreen). Both have the same specificity (one class plus one element), so the later li.done wins. Reversing them would turn the done items blue.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s07-positioning", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers the CSS position property and its four values (static, relative, absolute, fixed), what each is measured against, plus z-index stacking and drawing a circle with border-radius. The exercise positions a blue rectangle and nests a red circle inside it.</p>\n<h3>The position property and its offsets</h3>\n<ul><li>position takes static, relative, absolute or fixed. It is combined with the offset properties top, left, right and bottom to say how far the element moves.</li><li>An interactive demo (appbrewery.github.io/css-positioning) lets you switch between the values and inspect the generated CSS.</li><li>Offsets can use any side: right: 50px measures from the right edge instead. The browser works out the opposite side for you.</li><li>Position offsets are separate from margin, padding and width/height in the box model. Adding margin on top of an offset pushes the element further.</li></ul>\n<h3>static</h3>\n<ul><li>The default for every element. Items stack in the normal document flow, each one after the previous.</li><li>top, left and the other offsets have no effect on a static element, even if written.</li><li>Browser extensions that outline elements (such as Pesticide) help to see where each block sits in the flow.</li></ul>\n<h3>relative</h3>\n<ul><li>The element is shifted from where it would normally sit. The reference point is its own original location, not another element.</li><li>With position: relative; top: 50px; left: 50px; the box moves 50px down and 50px right from its normal spot.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The original space it occupied stays reserved, so siblings do not close up around the gap.</li><li>Setting relative on a parent with no offsets is the standard way of making it the reference frame for an absolute child.</li></ul>\n<h3>absolute</h3>\n<ul><li>Positions the element relative to its nearest ancestor that has a position value other than static. Only the closest positioned ancestor counts.</li><li>If no ancestor is positioned, the reference is the top-left of the page (the initial containing block).</li><li>Demonstrated in DevTools: adding position: relative via element.style on the parent makes the box jump to be offset from that parent instead of the page corner.</li><li>To place a child relative to a container, set the container to position: relative.</li><li>An absolute element leaves the normal flow and sits on its own layer above the others, so other content behaves as if it were not there.</li></ul>\n<h3>z-index</h3>\n<ul><li>Controls stacking along the third axis: a higher number is drawn above a lower number.</li><li>Everything defaults to 0. Negative values push an element behind others.</li><li>Setting z-index: 0 on an absolute element still does not return it to the flow; it remains on a separate layer. Only static or relative returns it to the normal flow.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> z-index only applies to elements whose position is not static (flex and grid items are an exception).</li></ul>\n<h3>fixed</h3>\n<ul><li>Positions relative to the browser window (viewport) rather than the page.</li><li>Looks the same as absolute until you scroll: a fixed element stays in the same spot on screen, while an absolute one scrolls with the page.</li><li>Typical uses (Extra): sticky headers, floating buttons and chat widgets.</li></ul>\n<h3>Drawing a circle</h3>\n<ul><li>border-radius rounds the corners of a box. 5px gives slight rounding and 50px a rounded square; a value of 50% on a square element produces a perfect circle.</li><li>The element still occupies a rectangular box in the box model even if it looks round. This matters for positioning: top: 50% and left: 50% place the circle's top-left corner at the centre of the parent, not the circle's middle.</li><li>A div needs an explicit width, height and background-color to be visible at all when it has no content.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution reasoning</h3>\n<ul><li>The blue box is made 500px wide and 300px tall. Its child circle has 200px by 200px, so the parent size must be set directly.</li><li>The blue box is moved with relative positioning (top and left 200px), which also makes it the reference frame for the circle. Absolute with no positioned ancestors would reach the same visual result.</li><li>The circle uses position: absolute with top: 150px and left: 250px. These equal half the parent's height and width, which is where the circle's top-left corner goes.</li><li>Many valid approaches exist, including making the blue box absolute or fixed since the page does not scroll. What matters is understanding what each choice does.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Relative: shifted from its normal position</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.box {\n  position: relative;\n  top: 50px;\n  left: 50px;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Absolute inside a relative parent, with z-index</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.parent { position: relative; }\n.child {\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 50px;    /* from the parent's top edge */\n  left: 50px;\n  z-index: -1;  /* sits behind other content */\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Fixed: stays put while scrolling</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.badge {\n  position: fixed;\n  top: 50px;\n  right: 50px;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Circle via border-radius</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.red-circle {\n  width: 200px;\n  height: 200px;\n  background-color: red;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: 7.3 CSS Positioning: rectangle with a circle</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html holds a single div.blue-box that contains a div.red-circle, plus an empty style block for your CSS.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Produce a blue 500x300 rectangle placed 200px from the top and left, with a 200px red circle inside it at 150px from the top and 250px from the left of the rectangle. Use position properties, not margins.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Give .red-circle background-color red, width and height 200px, and border-radius 50% so it becomes a circle.</li><li>Give .blue-box background-color blue, width 500px and height 300px.</li><li>Set .blue-box to position: relative with top: 200px and left: 200px.</li><li>Set .red-circle to position: absolute with top: 150px and left: 250px, so its offsets are measured from the blue box.</li><li>Compare with the goal image; the circle's top-left corner lands at the centre of the rectangle.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A blue rectangle offset 200px from the top-left of the page, with a red circle overlapping its middle, extending beyond the right and bottom edges of the rectangle.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.red-circle {\n  background-color: red;\n  width: 200px;\n  height: 200px;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 150px;\n  left: 250px;\n}\n\n.blue-box {\n  background-color: blue;\n  height: 300px;\n  width: 500px;\n  position: relative;\n  left: 200px;\n  top: 200px;\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Expecting relative to measure from another element; it is measured from the element's own normal position.</li><li>Forgetting position: relative on the parent, so the absolute child flies to the page's top-left corner.</li><li>Offsets on a static element do nothing.</li><li>Assuming z-index: 0 returns an absolute element to the flow.</li><li>Using 50% offsets and expecting the circle centred; that places its corner, not its centre. (Extra) Use transform: translate(-50%, -50%) to centre.</li><li>Leaving out width, height or background colour on an empty div, which makes it invisible.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("s07-flag", "<p class=\"summary\">Capstone for the Intermediate CSS section: rebuild the flag of Laos from a fixed, class-light HTML file using only CSS. It exercises combinators, specificity, the cascade and absolute/relative positioning. The walkthrough also shows Chrome's 'copy selector' as a shortcut for targeting unlabeled elements.</p>\n<h3>Why the project exists</h3>\n<ul><li>The intro showcases CSS art (a painted portrait and a Simpsons set built from divs with border-radius, width, height and colours) to show how far plain CSS goes. The flag is deliberately far smaller than those.</li><li>Constraint: index.html must not be edited. There are almost no classes or IDs, so the selectors have to come from structure (child and descendant combinators) and from positioning knowledge.</li><li>Skills being tested: combining selectors, specificity and cascade, and position relative/absolute. Exact placement is not graded; roughly matching the goal image is enough.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reading the HTML structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Outer div.flag is the red rectangle. Its children are a p (title text), then a div (blue rectangle).</li><li>Inside the blue div sits another div (the white circle), which contains a p with the second line of text.</li><li>So: .flag &gt; div selects the blue box, and .flag &gt; div &gt; div (or .flag div div) selects the circle. Both are structural selectors, not class-based.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Hints in the HTML comments give the flag size and circle size; the flag colours were read from the real flag image.</li></ul>\n<h3>Getting exact colours</h3>\n<ul><li>Inspect the flag image in Chrome DevTools, open the three-dot menu, More tools, CSS overview, then Capture overview. It lists the hex colours found (red #ce1126, blue #002868).</li><li>This re-uses the colour-picking technique from the earlier inspection lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step-by-step build</h3>\n<ul><li>Red rectangle: .flag gets width 900px, height 600px and the red background-color.</li><li>Blue rectangle: .flag &gt; div gets width 100% (equivalent to 900px, taken from the parent), height 300px (half the red box) and the blue background.</li><li>Circle: .flag &gt; div &gt; div gets width 200px, height 200px, white background, then border-radius 50% turns the square into a circle.</li><li>Layout of blue box: it should sit a quarter of the flag height (150px) down. Make .flag position: relative (it does not move; it becomes the reference) and the blue div position: absolute with top: 150px. This relative-parent / absolute-child pairing is the standard pattern.</li><li>Centre the circle in the blue box: blue is 900x300 and circle is 200x200, so top = (300-200)/2 = 50px and left = (900-200)/2 = 350px. For the offsets to refer to the blue box, the blue box itself must be a positioned element, which absolute positioning provides.</li><li>Why a naive centring looked off: the default margins on the p elements stayed in normal flow and pushed content down. Deleting them in DevTools fixed it, so the code sets p { margin: 0 } (padding 0 too in the solution).</li><li>Text: font-size 5rem on all p, text-align: center to centre both lines. The Pesticide extension / DevTools was used to see the paragraph box that caused the offset.</li></ul>\n<h3>Text colours and the 'copy selector' trick</h3>\n<ul><li>Easiest approach: set all p to white, then override only the circle's p to black. The default colour is already black, so alternatively only the top p could be made white.</li><li>Targeting the circle's p without classes: in the Elements panel, right-click the element, Copy, Copy selector. This yields a long chain like body &gt; div &gt; div &gt; div &gt; p.</li><li>Do not paste blindly. Understand it and simplify: drop the body, swap in .flag, and replace &gt; with spaces (descendant combinator), giving something like .flag div div p { color: black }.</li><li>This selector is more specific than the plain p rule, so it wins regardless of order (cascade and specificity).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final CSS for the Laos flag (cross-checked with the reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.flag {\n  width: 900px;\n  height: 600px;\n  position: relative;          /* reference point for the blue box */\n  background-color: #ce1126;\n}\n.flag &gt; div {                   /* blue band */\n  background-color: #002868;\n  width: 100%;\n  height: 300px;\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 150px;                   /* a quarter of 600px */\n}\n.flag &gt; div &gt; div {             /* white circle */\n  background-color: white;\n  width: 200px;\n  height: 200px;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  position: absolute;\n  top: 50px;                    /* (300 - 200) / 2 */\n  left: 350px;                  /* (900 - 200) / 2 */\n}\np {\n  font-size: 5rem;\n  color: white;\n  text-align: center;\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\n.flag &gt; div div p {\n  color: black;                 /* only the text inside the circle */\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Laos flag in pure CSS</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.html with a div.flag containing a p, a div, and inside that div another div holding a p. HTML is fixed and has almost no classes; a goal.png and solution.html are provided.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Using only CSS in the style block, recreate the flag: red field, blue band across the middle half, white circle centred in the band, a white heading line on top and black text inside the circle.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Size the .flag div 900x600 and colour it red (#ce1126).</li><li>Select the blue child div with .flag &gt; div, size it to 100% x 300px and colour it #002868.</li><li>Select the circle div (grandchild), size 200x200, white, border-radius 50%.</li><li>Make .flag position: relative; make the blue div absolute with top 150px; make the circle absolute with top 50px and left 350px.</li><li>Remove default paragraph margins and set font-size 5rem and text-align center.</li><li>Make the top text white and the circle's text black using a descendant selector.</li><li>Compare with solution.html (right-click, show preview).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A 900x600 flag: red top and bottom quarters, a blue band in the middle, a white circle in its centre with black text, white title text at the top.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.flag { width: 900px; height: 600px; position: relative; background-color: #ce1126; }\n.flag &gt; div { background-color: #002868; height: 300px; width: 100%; position: absolute; top: 150px; }\n.flag &gt; div &gt; div { background-color: white; position: absolute; height: 200px; width: 200px; border-radius: 50%; top: 50px; left: 350px; }\np { font-size: 5rem; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 0; margin: 0; }\n.flag &gt; div div p { color: black; }</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting position: relative on the parent, so absolute offsets are measured from the page instead of from the flag.</li><li>Leaving default p margins, which shift the content and make the circle look off-centre.</li><li>Pasting a long copied selector without understanding it; simplify it so it stays readable.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The repo's index.html is a student's own attempt (relative blue box at top: 60px, author footer), not the starter; use solution.html as the reference and ignore the 'Made with' footer.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l047", "<p class=\"summary\">A short motivational check-in at the end of the Intermediate CSS module. The message is that programming ability comes from sustained practice rather than innate talent.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Feeling 'not cut out' for coding is common; programming skill is built through repeated practice, not inherited.</li><li>Skills that look effortless in others usually hide a large amount of unseen work, and apparent overnight successes tend to follow many earlier attempts.</li><li>Progress comes from showing up regularly and putting in hours; results earned this way feel more valuable.</li><li>Practical takeaway: keep going through the exercises and projects even when CSS positioning feels frustrating.</li></ul>");
